          "changeMeaning": "Initialize the lab and display the linear molecular geometry of carbon dioxide as the first example of steric number 2.",
          "narration": "Welcome to the VSEPR Theory lab. Valence Shell Electron Pair Repulsion theory states that electron pairs around a central atom repel each other, adjusting their 3D positions to be as far apart as possible. Our first example is beryllium chloride or carbon dioxide, representing a steric number of 2. The two bonding domains point in opposite directions, creating a linear geometry with a perfect 180-degree bond angle."
        },
        {
          "clipName": "clip1_trigonal_planar",
          "audioClip": "vsepr_trigonal_planar",
          "autoAdvance": false,
          "changes": [
            {
              "target": "subtitle_label",
              "components": [
                {
                  "componentType": "textMeshPro",
                  "text": "Trigonal Planar (Steric 3) - Angle: 120\u00b0",
                  "fontSize": 30
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "target": "linear_mol",
              "enabled": false
            },
            {
              "target": "trig_planar_mol",
              "enabled": true,
              "components": [
                {
                  "componentType": "simpleRotation",
                  "timeRate": 1.0,
                  "rotationTime": 15
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "changeMeaning": "Disable linear molecule and show the trigonal planar geometry of boron trifluoride representing steric number 3.",
          "narration": "When we increase the steric number to 3, as in boron trifluoride, we introduce a third electron domain. To minimize mutual repulsion, the three domains arrange themselves in a single flat plane, pointing towards the corners of an equilateral triangle. This is trigonal planar geometry, featuring ideal 120-degree bond angles."
        },
        {
          "clipName": "clip2_tetrahedral",
          "audioClip": "vsepr_tetrahedral",
          "autoAdvance": false,
          "changes": [
            {
              "target": "subtitle_label",
              "components": [
                {
                  "componentType": "textMeshPro",
                  "text": "Tetrahedral Geometry (Steric 4) - Angle: 109.5\u00b0",
                  "fontSize": 30
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "target": "trig_planar_mol",
              "enabled": false
            },
            {
              "target": "tetrahedral_mol",
              "enabled": true,
              "components": [
                {
                  "componentType": "simpleRotation",
                  "timeRate": 1.0,
                  "rotationTime": 15
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "changeMeaning": "Disable trigonal planar molecule and show the tetrahedral geometry of methane representing steric number 4.",
          "narration": "With a steric number of 4, like in methane, the domains can no longer stay in a flat 2D plane to optimize their distances. They expand into three dimensions, pointing toward the vertices of a tetrahedron. This is tetrahedral geometry, with ideal 109.5-degree bond angles. Observe how the molecule rotates to show its perfectly uniform 3D spacing."
        }
      ]
