              ]
            }
          ],
          "changeMeaning": "Enable ammonia in the center to show lone pair compression and the resulting trigonal pyramidal shape.",
          "narration": "Next, look at ammonia in the center. It has three bonding pairs and one non-bonding lone pair, highlighted here as a translucent cloud. Because a lone pair is attracted to only one nucleus, its electron cloud spreads out more than bonding pairs. This increased repulsion squeezes the N-H bonds closer together, reducing the bond angle to 107 degrees and forming a trigonal pyramidal shape."
        },
