          "changeMeaning": "Disable the three steric-4 comparison molecules and show XeF4 to demonstrate square planar geometry in steric 6 with 2 lone pairs.",
          "narration": "Finally, what happens to lone pairs in expanded geometries? In xenon tetrafluoride, with a steric number of 6, we have four bonding pairs and two lone pairs. To minimize repulsion, the two lone pairs occupy axial positions directly opposite each other, 180 degrees apart. This allows their strong repulsions to balance out, leaving the four fluorine atoms in a perfectly flat square planar molecular geometry with 90-degree angles."
        }
